schema: claim-evidence
object:
  title: "Founder/team conflict is a leading cause of startup failure (Wasserman)"
  type: claim-evidence
  claim: >-
    Harvard Business School's Noam Wasserman, studying roughly 10,000
    founders for "The Founder's Dilemmas," found that founder-related issues
    (disputes over roles, equity, and commitment) are a leading cause of
    startup failure, with co-founder conflict frequently cited as derailing
    a large share of otherwise promising ventures.
  evidence: >-
    Cited in the article from Noam Wasserman, "The Founder's Dilemmas"
    (Princeton University Press), listed in the article's References with a
    link to the publisher's page.
  interpretation: >-
    Used by the article to support its core thesis: "who you build with, and
    how clearly you agree on terms, decides more than your strategy does" —
    an argument for small, trust-bonded founding teams over headcount.
  uncertainty: >-
    Reproduces the article's own paraphrase of Wasserman's research; the
    precise share of failures attributed to founder conflict is described in
    the article only as "a large share," not a specific cited percentage.
    Not independently verified against the book for this ingestion.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/building-founding-team.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  work_order: wo-f2680cdb2876
  source_lineage: building-founding-team.md
